- op: discuss  # 082a
  at: '2026-08-30T11:14:00Z'  # 082a
  by: agent  # 082a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 082a
  clock: 4  # 082a
  nonce: 082a  # 082a
  message: "SHAPE A SHIPPED (dev 0a35d4bc73). The item's predicted fix was right: Strategy\n1.5 resolves the self.field chain through the struct field-type registry and then\nuses the type ONLY for a first-party symbol lookup, so an EXTERNAL field type is\ncomputed and discarded. Recovered into the module slot -- slot only,\nhas_explicit_binding untouched, so emission is byte-identical. rust.py at 100%\ncoverage; 5053 mainstream + 13446 core tests green.\n\n*** THE ITEM'S OWN SIZING NEEDS A CAVEAT, AND IT IS THE SAME ONE INV-bagok IS\nBUILT ON. *** This item reads \"write_all matches 5 catalogued method sites and\nMISSES 125\" and \"lock is the single largest miss in the cohort at 440\". Those\ncount SYNTACTIC misses. Only the subset whose receiver type actually IS the\ncatalogued type is recoverable, and on spacedrive that subset is small:\n\n  receiver-shape census, spacedrive (1,097 .rs files)\n    write_all   self.<field>  4     any.<field>  4     bare ident  94\n    lock        self.<field> 74     any.<field> 87     bare ident  64\n    write       self.<field>295     any.<field>329     bare ident  67\n\n`lock` is catalogued ONLY as std::fs::File.lock. Spacedrive's 74 are Mutex::lock.\nClassifying them would be a FALSE POSITIVE, not a recovered true positive -- a\nname matching is not the same as the boundary being true. Same for a field\n`.write` on a Vec or a custom writer. So 125/440 is an upper bound on POPULATION,\nnot on payoff, and the next person sizing this work should not inherit the larger\nfigure. The fix is type-directed and fires exactly when the field's type resolves\nto the catalogued one, which is why the measured delta is small BY DESIGN.\n\n*** A/B, two Rust repositories, one process, pre-fix arm restored by blanking the\nfield-type registry so Strategy 1.5 resolves nothing: ***\n\n  spacedrive             587 -> 593 classified call sites  (+5 fs_write, +1 fs_read)\n  encrypted-dns-server     9 ->   9                         (+0)\n\nencrypted-dns-server's zero is kept rather than dropped: it is the repo rust.py's\nown comment cites for PR #595 (\"203 of 219 method-construct edges carried the bare\nexternal placeholder\"), so it is the natural place to look for effect, and it\nclassifies 9 sites in TOTAL -- almost no catalogued surface left for this to\nreach. Reported as a CEILING ON THE FINDING DELTA, NOT A PAYOFF: this item's\nstanding rule is L4-on-findings, and a call site that does not classify cannot\nproduce a finding, so this bounds the direction without establishing the size. I\ndid NOT run the taint arm, so the finding delta is unmeasured.\n\n*** SHAPE B IS NOT WHAT THIS ITEM'S DIAGNOSIS IMPLIES, AND IT IS STILL OPEN. ***\nThe description's example is `h.f.write_all(...)`, diagnosed as \"the receiver node\nis a field_expression rather than an identifier and _var_types is keyed by\nvariable name\". True -- but the deeper constraint is that\nanalyze/base.py's resolve_receiver_type resolves \"only ... chains rooted at a\nself_keywords token. Non-self receivers return None.\" So Strategy 1.5 never fires\nfor a parameter-rooted chain at all, and reusing it cannot reach shape B. What\nshape B needs is a VAR-ROOTED field-chain walk -- which Go already has\n(_resolve_field_chain, root in var_types then walk the field registry) and Rust\ndoes not. That is a change in SHARED analyze/base.py or a Rust-local walker, with\na wider blast radius than this fix, so it is left open here and asserted at its\ncurrent behaviour in test_rust_field_receiver_module_slot.py rather than omitted.\n\nAlso still open and NOT this item: a receiver bound from an external function's\nRETURN VALUE (`let c = File::create(..).unwrap()`) is WI-lalot.\n\n*** ONE FIXTURE HAZARD WORTH THE NEXT PERSON'S TIME. *** Rust's _var_types is\nFILE-scoped and first-writer-wins, so a receiver named `f` in one function is\ntyped by an annotation on a DIFFERENT function's `f`. The first cut of the fixture\nused `f` everywhere; a broken shape appeared to pass because a later function's\n`d: &mut File` had already typed the name. Every receiver in the shipped fixture\nhas a distinct name, and its line numbers are DERIVED from the source rather than\nhand-counted -- the first cut hardcoded five and got one wrong by one, which\nsurfaced as a CONTROL failing and looked exactly like the fix breaking PR #595.\n\nRig: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/dizag_rust_08302026/"  # 082a
